  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 142 AM EDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nSaturday and Sunday will feature decreasing heights aloft as the \nupper ridge, centered over the Arklatex region, begins to break \ndown. That will place thee southern Appalachian region on the \nperiphery of the retreating ridge, allowing for a mix of diurnally \nforced convection and thunderstorm complexes moving in from the \nnorthwest in this \"Ring of Fire\" type setup. Much of the CWA is in a \nMarginal risk area for a damaging wind threat tomorrow, while only \nin a general thunder area on SPC's Day 3 outlook. I wouldn't be \nsurprised to see that expanded on Sunday. The thermodynamic setup \nfor both Saturday and Sunday looks similar to today, that is to say \nDCAPE values in the 1,000 - 1,200 J/kg range, favorable lapse rates, \nand MLCAPE north of 1,500 J/kg. As such, it would not be hard to \nimagine a scenario where a handful of Sig Wx Advisories and a maybe \na couple of severe thunderstorm warnings are issued. \n\nLooking ahead to Monday and Tuesday, afternoon showers and storms \nremain in the forecast as the ridge continues to break down and an \nupper low, once over the Intermountain West, shifts east across the \nplains and into the Great Lakes region Sunday through Tuesday. \nTemperatures will remain well above normal through this time.\n\nTuesday night into Wednesday the upper low deepens over the Great \nLakes region and leads to a pattern change characterized by much \nmore notable troughing over the eastern CONUS as opposed to high \npressure. In fact, we may have a legitimate cold front passage next \nWednesday beneath the upper trough/low. In addition to increased \nchances for showers and storms this pattern will bring, temperatures \nwill begin cooling off behind on the backside of the trough. Drier \nand cooler conditions expected on Thursday and Friday. High \ntemperatures will be in the 70s/80s as opposed to the 80s/90s. \nOvernight lows will be in the 50s/60s instead of the 60s/70s. So \nhold on for a few more days of heat, relief is on the way! \n\nLastly, until we see a relief in temps, Chattanooga will continue to \nbuild onto their record streak of 90 degrees or higher. They \ncurrently sit at 45 days, with several more days likely to add to \nthe streak. The first day where the official forecast is below 90 is \nnext Wednesday. If they hold on to 90 through Tuesday, that would \nbring the streak up to 50 days.",
